s directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source A, titled Voyager 2 at Uranus, states that mission planners directed the spacecraft to Uranus, and source B confirms Voyager 2 flew by Uranus.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-003\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"a journey that would take about 4,5 years\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source A states the journey to Uranus would take about 4,5 years.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-004\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says the Jupiter encounter was optimized, not the Saturn encounter.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-005\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"The Ur anus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says the future encounter was with Saturn, not Neptune.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-006\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives 6.4 days of close study, not 5.5 hours.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-007\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 1's\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source names Voyager 1 as the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, not Voyager 2.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-008\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives short-range observations beginning Jan. 31, 1987, not long-range observations on Nov. 4, 1985.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-009\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"began Jan. 31, 1987, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source ties the 2,5-hour signal time to Jan. 31, 1987, not Nov. 4, 1985.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-010\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says five-hundred times less, not 400 times.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-011\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives 17:59 ED on Jan. 24, 1968, which differs from 17:59 UTC on Jan. 24, 1986 in both time zone and year.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-012\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles), so the claim swaps the units.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-013\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says 11 new moons, not 10.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-014\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives different names, including Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Cordelina and Bianca II.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-015\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"obvious allusions to Goethe\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says the names allude to Goethe, not Shakespeare.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-016\",\"verdict\":\"MISSING\",\"evidence\":\"\",\"evidence_source\":\"\",\"rationale\":\"Neither source mentions Titania and Oberon as the start of the naming tradition; source B only says a tradition began in 1687.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-017\",\"verdict\":\"MISSING\",\"evidence\":\"\",\"evidence_source\":\"\",\"rationale\":\"Neither source states when Titania and Oberon were discovered.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-018\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"three new rings\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says three new rings, not two.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-019\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"the “older” eight rings\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says there were eight older rings, not nine.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-020\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says the tilt is 66 degrees, not 59.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-021\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source B states the magnetic field is off-center.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-022\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour), not 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour).\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-023\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source describes a lake at 479 miles (900 kilometers), not an ocean at 497 miles (800 kilometers).\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-024\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source B states the rings were extremely variable in thickness.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-025\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source B states the rings were extremely variable in transparency.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-026\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source names Ariele, Umbrella and Titan rather than Ariel, Umbriel and Titania.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-027\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"five of Uranus’ smaller moons\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source calls them Uranus' smaller moons, not its larger moons.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-028\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers)\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source B states the Miranda flyby range exactly as claimed.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-029\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source B states the Miranda flyby was the closest approach to any object so far.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-030\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"its nearly century-long travels\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source describes the travels as nearly century-long, not nearly a decade.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-031\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source B states the Miranda images showed a surface that was a mishmash of peculiar features.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-032\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source B states Uranus appeared generally featureless.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-033\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"Source B states the Uranus news was interrupted the same day by the Challenger accident.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-034\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"killed six astronauts\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says six astronauts were killed, not seven.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-035\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives the launch date as Feb. 28, 1986, not Jan. 28, 1986.\"}]}","ttft_ms":17128,"type":"result","duration_ms":40036,"uuid":"bf5960fe-bbce-4e89-8fc3-bffecab96aa4","ttft_stream_ms":1316,"time_to_request_ms":119,"first_content_frame_ms":1316,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
